{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792935215,"nonce":"m18x1OD5eR7IIk39h0bcMxnnCEI=","title":"A host's var-args closure entry is called without a C trap, so a throw from it skips the interpreted try","message":"crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/ops.rs, CallClosure, the fun == fn_fun_var_args branch: when the wrapped fun is neither a function nor a native of the program, it is a C entry a host wrapped with hlp_make_var_args, and it is called directly, f((*wrapped).value, array). The record-closure branch just below runs its entry under run_with_hl_trap and turns a longjmp into the exception with longjmp_error (stack depth), as call_native does for every bytecode native.\n\nSo when such an entry calls hlp_throw, no trap exists for the interpreted try around the call: the throw longjmps to the outermost C trap, or with none prints 'hlp_throw called without active trap; aborting' and the process dies. The guest's error never reaches the Haxe catch.\n\nReported by the caribou session. Shape: ChimpMonk calls each registered system as try s.fn(this, dt) catch (e:Dynamic); a Wren/Lua/Python function registered as a system aborts the process when it raises, in interp and hybrid. Caribou works around it for typed function parameters (record closure); a function held as Dynamic still becomes a var-args closure over a C entry and still hits this. Any HDLL that makes var-args closures over C entries would hit the same.\n\nFix: run the host-entry call in that branch under run_with_hl_trap and map a nonzero result with longjmp_error, as the record-closure branch does. Test: extend crates/ash_interp/tests/host_module.rs (already builds a var-args closure over adder_entry and runs per tier mode in a child process) with an entry that throws, called inside a Haxe try.\n\nAlso check the other places a var-args or host closure is called directly (compiled tiers, hlp_call_method) for the same gap.","files":null}]}